          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em monomorphism
            is the generalization of the notion of
            %em injective map of sets
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            to arbitrary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} categories
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} formally dual
            concept is that of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective+function"} surjective function
          %p Common jargon includes “is a mono” or “is monic” for “is a monomorphism”, and “is an epi” or “is epic” for “is an epimorphism”, and “is an iso” for “is an isomorphism”.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon X \to Y
            in some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            is called a
            %em monomorphism
            (sometimes abbrieviated to
            = succeed "," do
              %em mono
            = succeed ")," do
              %em monic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            and every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+of+parallel+morphisms"} pair of parallel morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1,g_2 \colon Z \to X
            then
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 2
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 2
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  f \circ g_1
                  \\,=\,
                  f \circ g_2
                  \\right)
                  \\;\Rightarrow \;
                  \\left(
                  g_1 \,=\, g_2
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Stated more abstractly, this says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a monomorphism if for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(Z,-)
            takes it to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+function"} injective function
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-sets"} hom-sets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %mphantom
                        %mi AA
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mphantom
                        %mi AA
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Hom(Z,X)
                  \\overset{\phantom{AA} f_\ast \phantom{AA}}{\hookrightarrow}
                  Hom(Z,Y)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since injective functions are precisely the monomorphisms in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MonomorphismsInSet"}
            below) this may be stated as saying that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a monomorphism if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(Z,f)
            is a monomorphism for all objects
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
          %p
            Finally,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            being a monomorphism in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            means equivalently that it is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{op}
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#general General
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %strong (monomorphisms in preorders)
            %p
              In a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
          #MonomorphismsInSet.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (monomorphisms in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %p
              The monomorphisms in the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
              between them are precisely the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+functions"} injective functions
          #MonomorphismsInCat.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (monomorphisms in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %p
              The monomorphisms in the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
              between them are precisely the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/embeddings+of+categories"} embeddings of categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective-on-objects+functor"} injective-on-objects
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functors"} faithful functors
          .num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
              is both a monomorphism and an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
          %p But beware that the converse fails:
          %h3#ExamplesOfMonosThatAreEpiButNotIso Examples of monos that are epi but not iso
          %p
            The following lists some examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            that are both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
            %p
              In the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+spaces"} Hausdorff topological spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow X
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subspace"} dense subspace
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            %p
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "Hausdorff+space#DenseSubspaceInclusionsAreEpimorphismsAmongHausdorffSpaces"} this Prop.
            for proof.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+ring"} unital
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rings"} Rings
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi i
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \overset{i}{\hookrightarrow} \mathbb{Q}
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            %p
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "Ring#InclusionOfIntegersIntoRationalsIsEpimorphismOfRings"} this Prop.
            for proof.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            We list the following properties without their (easy) proofs. The proofs can be found spelled out (dually) at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
          %p
          #BasicCharacterizationOfMonomorphisms.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p The following are equivalent:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi x
                        %mo →
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : x \to y
                  is a monomorphism in
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
                  in the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mi op
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
              %li
                %p
                  postcomposition with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
                  in
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in C
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo :
                        %mi Hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi Hom
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi c
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \circ -: Hom(c,x) \to Hom(c,y)
                  is an
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injection"} injection
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi x
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mi Id
                              %mtd
                                %mi x
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mrow
                                  %mi Id
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msup
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                  %mi f
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi x
                              %mtd
                                %munder
                                  %mo →
                                  %mi f
                              %mtd
                                %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        x &amp;\stackrel{Id}{\to}&amp; x
                        \\\
                        {}^{Id}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{f}
                        \\\
                        x &amp;\underset{f}{\to}&amp; y
                        }
                %p
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                  diagram.
            %p
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon x \to y
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi y
                    %mo →
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \colon y \to z
              are monomorphisms, so is their composite
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g f
              is a monomorphism, so is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ⟶
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    t \to x \stackrel{\longrightarrow}{\longrightarrow} y
            %p is a monomorphism.
          %p
            The converse of the above proposition fails, and a mononomorphism that is the equalizer of some pair of morphisms is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular monomorphism
          #MonomorphismsArePreservedByPullback.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Monomorphisms are preserved by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          %p
            (In an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adhesive+category"} adhesive category
            they are also preserved by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Monomorphisms are preserved by any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              Monomorphisms are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflected+limit"} reflected
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functors"} faithful functors
          %p We have seen some ways in which monomorphisms get along with limits. Here is another:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p Any morphism from a terminal object is a monomorphism. The product of monomorphisms is a monomorphism.
          %p
            Monomorphisms do not get along quite as well with colimits. For example, the unique morphism from the initial object is not always a monomorphism, and the canonical morphisms from the summands into a coproduct, e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_1 : x_1 \to x_1 + x_2
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+initial+object"} strict
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+coproduct"} disjoint coproduct
          %h2#variations Variations
          %p
            At
            %a{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism#Variations"} epimorphism
            there is a long list of variations on the concept of epimorphism. Each of these, of course, has a dual notion for monomorphism, but the ones most commonly used are:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+monomorphism"} split monomorphism
              \= morphism which has a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retraction"} retraction
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+monomorphism"} normal monomorphism
              \= a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel"} kernel
              of some morphism (in a category with
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+morphisms"} zero morphisms
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+monomorphism"} regular monomorphism
              \= an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
              of some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+pair"} parallel pair
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monomorphism"} strong monomorphism
              \= a monomorphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonality"} right orthogonal
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphisms"} epimorphisms
            %li monomorphism
          %p
            Frequently, regular and strong monos coincide. For instance, this is the case in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopos"} quasitopos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
          %p
            Sometimes, all monomorphisms are regular—this seems to happen a bit more frequently than for epimorphisms. For instance, this is the case in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            (including any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            and in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroups"} normal subgroups
            (hence the name). In any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab-enriched+category"} Ab-enriched category
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            (in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classical mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
            domain is split. Of course, no mono with empty domain and inhabited codomain can be split (in contrast to the dual case, where it can happen that all epimorphisms split – the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                classes of monomorphism define
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobjects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"}> monomorphism in an (∞,1)-category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-monomorphism"} n-monomorphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"}> image
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coimage"} coimage
            %li
              %p
                a category in which all morphisms are monomorphisms is called a
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+cancellative+category"} left cancellative category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jointly+monic+morphisms"} jointly monic morphisms
          %h2#references References
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                , §I.5 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
                %strong 5
                Springer (1971, second ed. 1997) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                , Section 1.7 in:
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                Vol. 1:
                %em Basic Category Theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511525858"}> doi:10.1017/CBO9780511525858
                \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
